Now the review of your aiming strategy.

1. The "paradox" is mostly two different lines, and a smaller effect than target motion

The two lines: the bomb falls below the flight-path line, so the flight path must point beyond the target. The pilot's sight line goes through the target itself. These are different lines and don't conflict. Real sights, like the Ju87's, were set up with a depression offset for this.
Size of the offset (150 m/s, bomb with light drag):
Release height	Dive angle	Impact short of the flight-line intercept	Sight-line angle to the path
610 m (2,000 ft)	60°	48 m	3.5°
610 m	70°	27 m	2.2°
610 m	80°	12 m	1.1°
457 m (1,500 ft)	70°	16 m	1.8°

A steeper dive and a lower release shrink the offset, which is why Stuka pilots dived steeply and released low.

Target motion matters more: at 20 m/s a target moves about 60–80 m during the 3–4 s fall, and about 600 m during the 30 s dive. So the dive geometry is dominated by target motion, not by the ballistic offset.
Approach from behind: this makes the problem one-dimensional along the target's track. Target motion then becomes a timing term for the push-down rather than a cross-track error.
How to manage it: aim the predicted impact point, not the flight path, as modern HUDs do with a continuously computed impact point. Release with the planned dive at its end (your option 1), and require the impact prediction to land on the target's predicted position at impact time. The aiming error then splits into along-track (corrected by dive angle) and cross-track (corrected by heading).

2. Targeting and aiming

Useful difference: yes. The better boundary is the push-down, because that is the commit point.
Targeting (your phases 1–3): reversible, so you can abort, go around and try again, at any time.
Aiming (your phase 4): committed. After the push-down the only abort is an earlier pull-out, and the aircraft is losing height.
Different controls: targeting uses large manoeuvres and low sensitivity. Aiming uses small corrections of a few degrees in dive angle and heading, and its abort rule is a height-based gate.

3. Phases missing or not needed

Add phase 0, acquire and validate: check that the target record is valid, its speed is below 40 kn and it is within range. Then capture the entry altitude (using start_climb if needed) and the entry speed. The dive predictor must pass before the commit.
Phases 1 and 2 are positioning in 2D, not only heading: heading alone leaves a lateral offset. They need cross-track and along-track range as well. A funnel on heading error and cross-track error, narrowing as the push-down point nears, would cover this.
Add a decision gate before release: if the predicted miss is too large, do the pull-out without releasing. Also add a go-around path after any abort.
Release is an event: it creates a projectile that the sim steps to the ground. That gives a measured miss distance for testing.
Not needed: your option 2 (release in the early pull-up), and a separate "dive angle from line of sight" step. The dive angle follows from tracking the impact point.
Later: an egress turn after the pull-out.

4. Abort criteria

Stages 1–2 (approach):
the target record is invalid or stale
the target speed is above the limit
the approach timer expires
the aircraft is below the safe entry height, or its speed is too low
the aircraft reaches the push-down point outside the funnel, which means a go-around and re-approach, with an attempt limit
Stage 3 (push-down): the same checks, plus predict_dive refusing, and the predicted correctable miss exceeding the limit.
After the commit: abort means pulling out without release, either at the decision gate or at the base-clearance limit already in predict_dive.

5. Simplest target API

One record, target: {lat, lon, alt_ft, hdg, spd_kt}, passed in the options. Named fields avoid mixing up the lon/lat order. The code extrapolates it by dead reckoning at constant velocity.
A property path, target: "/ai/models/ship[0]", is an alternative that reads the live AI object each tick.
stationary_threshold_kt defaults to 2 kt, and anything above 40 kn is refused.
ground_ft defaults to the target's altitude, so the pull-out height is measured above the target.

Programming order (v0.7) — build backwards, so each phase's entry requirements come from the one after it:

v0.7.0: target record, tracker and local east/north geometry helpers, projectile integrator (with simple drag), and impact-point calculation. Test the impact point against a closed-form fall.
v0.7.1: extend predict_dive to return ground distance and time, and add the aiming-error function and the push-down trigger for a stationary target aligned with the approach. Open loop, to measure dispersion.
v0.7.2: aiming corrections in the dive: dive angle for along-track error, heading for cross-track error, tested with offsets and a moving target.
v0.7.3: phases 0–2: approach line, funnel, aborts, go-around.
v0.7.4: moving targets (circling to approach from behind) and a bench Monte Carlo giving the miss distance for random starts.

Things to watch

Predictor cost: predict_dive takes much longer than the 150 µs tick. I'd run it at about 2 Hz.
Heading authority in a steep dive: the heading rate for a given bank is about 3× higher at 70° (1/cos γ). I'll check update_roll_target for that.
Pushover time: at n_push of 0 the push-down takes about 23 s, so the prediction horizon is about 35 s. That sets how far a moving target travels and how far out the dive starts. With −1 it is about 9 s.

